1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,845
Welcome to Unit Five of Algebra Two:
polynomial functions. We start with how a

2
00:00:05,845 --> 00:00:09,067
polynomial's graph behaves at its far
ends.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:15,573
A polynomial function is a sum of terms
like a x to the n, with whole-number

4
00:00:15,573 --> 00:00:16,280
exponents.

5
00:00:16,730 --> 00:00:21,609
The degree is the highest exponent, and
the leading coefficient is the number in

6
00:00:21,609 --> 00:00:22,767
front of that term.

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:27,036
When the degree is even, both ends go the
same way.

8
00:00:27,486 --> 00:00:33,141
For x squared or x to the fourth with a
positive leading coefficient, both ends

9
00:00:33,141 --> 00:00:36,147
rise. With a negative one, both ends fall.

10
00:00:37,267 --> 00:00:40,253
When the degree is odd, the ends go
opposite ways.

11
00:00:40,703 --> 00:00:46,100
For x cubed with a positive leading
coefficient, the left end falls and the

12
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:49,770
right end rises. A negative coefficient
flips that.

13
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:54,143
So for end behavior, you only need the
leading term.

14
00:00:54,593 --> 00:00:59,955
For f of x equals negative two x to the
fifth plus x squared, the degree is odd

15
00:00:59,955 --> 00:01:05,385
and the coefficient is negative, so the
graph rises on the left and falls on the

16
00:01:05,385 --> 00:01:05,793
right.

17
00:01:06,933 --> 00:01:12,898
A polynomial of degree n has at most n
minus one turning points, and at most n

18
00:01:12,898 --> 00:01:13,739
real zeros.

19
00:01:14,189 --> 00:01:16,066
A cubic can turn at most twice.

20
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:23,349
Your turn. Describe the end behavior of f
of x equals negative x to the fourth plus

21
00:01:23,349 --> 00:01:23,941
three x.

22
00:01:24,391 --> 00:01:25,927
Pause the video and try it.

23
00:01:26,377 --> 00:01:31,125
The degree is even and the leading
coefficient is negative, so both ends

24
00:01:31,125 --> 00:01:31,455
fall.

25
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:37,941
Polynomials can be divided just like
numbers. In this lesson, we'll use long

26
00:01:37,941 --> 00:01:38,573
division.

27
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:45,198
The process repeats three steps: divide
the leading terms, multiply, and subtract.

28
00:01:45,198 --> 00:01:47,209
Then bring down the next term.

29
00:01:48,333 --> 00:01:52,323
Divide x squared plus five x plus six by x
plus two.

30
00:01:52,773 --> 00:01:59,962
x squared divided by x is x. Multiply: x
squared plus two x. Subtract: three x.

31
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:07,513
Bring down the six: three x plus six.
Divide: three. Multiply: three x plus six.

32
00:02:07,513 --> 00:02:08,716
Subtract: zero.

33
00:02:09,166 --> 00:02:12,430
So the quotient is x plus three, with no
remainder.

34
00:02:13,567 --> 00:02:17,151
When the remainder isn't zero, write it
over the divisor.

35
00:02:17,601 --> 00:02:23,910
Two x squared plus three x minus four,
divided by x minus one, gives two x plus

36
00:02:23,910 --> 00:02:30,059
five, with remainder one. So it's two x
plus five, plus one over x minus one.

37
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:34,485
If a power is missing, insert a zero
placeholder.

38
00:02:34,935 --> 00:02:41,375
To divide x cubed minus eight by x minus
two, write x cubed plus zero x squared

39
00:02:41,375 --> 00:02:47,245
plus zero x minus eight. The quotient is x
squared plus two x plus four.

40
00:02:48,367 --> 00:02:53,359
Your turn. Divide x squared minus x minus
twelve by x minus four.

41
00:02:53,809 --> 00:02:55,345
Pause the video and try it.

42
00:02:55,795 --> 00:02:58,973
The quotient is x plus three, with
remainder zero.

43
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:04,857
Synthetic division is a fast shortcut for
dividing by x minus c.

44
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,914
It only works when the divisor is linear
with leading coefficient one, like x minus

45
00:03:11,914 --> 00:03:13,339
three or x plus two.

46
00:03:14,467 --> 00:03:19,800
Divide x cubed minus four x squared plus x
plus six by x minus two.

47
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:25,914
Write c equals two on the left, and the
coefficients one, negative four, one, six

48
00:03:25,914 --> 00:03:26,543
in a row.

49
00:03:27,667 --> 00:03:33,405
Bring down the one. Multiply by two and
add to the next coefficient: negative four

50
00:03:33,405 --> 00:03:35,155
plus two is negative two.

51
00:03:35,605 --> 00:03:41,945
Repeat: negative two times two is negative
four, plus one is negative three. Negative

52
00:03:41,945 --> 00:03:45,674
three times two is negative six, plus six
is zero.

53
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:52,281
The bottom row, one, negative two,
negative three, is the quotient: x squared

54
00:03:52,281 --> 00:03:53,989
minus two x minus three.

55
00:03:54,439 --> 00:03:56,786
The last number, zero, is the remainder.

56
00:03:57,933 --> 00:03:59,875
Missing terms still need zeros.

57
00:04:00,325 --> 00:04:06,543
For x cubed minus twenty-seven divided by
x minus three, use one, zero, zero,

58
00:04:06,543 --> 00:04:12,357
negative twenty-seven. The quotient is x
squared plus three x plus nine.

59
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:19,482
Your turn. Use synthetic division to
divide x squared plus three x minus ten by

60
00:04:19,482 --> 00:04:20,391
x plus five.

61
00:04:20,841 --> 00:04:22,377
Pause the video and try it.

62
00:04:22,827 --> 00:04:29,471
c is negative five. The bottom row is one,
negative two, zero. So the quotient is x

63
00:04:29,471 --> 00:04:31,552
minus two, remainder zero.

64
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:37,666
Synthetic division does more than divide.
In this lesson, we'll see what the

65
00:04:37,666 --> 00:04:38,908
remainder tells us.

66
00:04:40,033 --> 00:04:46,279
The remainder theorem: when you divide f
of x by x minus c, the remainder is f of

67
00:04:46,279 --> 00:04:46,433
c.

68
00:04:46,883 --> 00:04:49,145
So dividing is a way to evaluate.

69
00:04:50,267 --> 00:04:57,008
Find f of three for f of x equals two x
cubed minus five x squared plus x minus

70
00:04:57,008 --> 00:04:57,435
four.

71
00:04:57,885 --> 00:05:03,271
Synthetic division with three gives a
bottom row of two, one, four, eight. So f

72
00:05:03,271 --> 00:05:04,498
of three is eight.

73
00:05:05,633 --> 00:05:12,012
The factor theorem: x minus c is a factor
of f exactly when f of c equals zero.

74
00:05:12,462 --> 00:05:18,800
Is x plus one a factor of x cubed plus x
squared minus four x minus four? f of

75
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:25,219
negative one is negative one plus one plus
four minus four, which is zero. Yes.

76
00:05:26,367 --> 00:05:29,332
Divide to get the rest: x squared minus
four.

77
00:05:29,782 --> 00:05:36,437
So the polynomial factors completely as x
plus one, times x plus two, times x minus

78
00:05:36,437 --> 00:05:36,758
two.

79
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:43,639
Find k so that x minus two is a factor of
x cubed plus k x minus six.

80
00:05:44,089 --> 00:05:50,482
Set f of two equal to zero: eight plus two
k minus six equals zero, so k equals

81
00:05:50,482 --> 00:05:51,534
negative one.

82
00:05:52,667 --> 00:05:58,822
Your turn. Is x minus three a factor of x
cubed minus two x squared minus five x

83
00:05:58,822 --> 00:05:59,515
plus six?

84
00:05:59,965 --> 00:06:01,501
Pause the video and try it.

85
00:06:01,951 --> 00:06:07,967
f of three is twenty-seven minus eighteen
minus fifteen plus six, which is zero.

86
00:06:07,967 --> 00:06:09,396
Yes, it's a factor.

87
00:06:10,533 --> 00:06:15,808
How do you find the zeros of a cubic or
quartic? In this lesson, we'll use the

88
00:06:15,808 --> 00:06:17,296
rational root theorem.

89
00:06:18,433 --> 00:06:23,922
If a polynomial has integer coefficients,
any rational zero is a factor of the

90
00:06:23,922 --> 00:06:27,862
constant term, over a factor of the
leading coefficient.

91
00:06:28,312 --> 00:06:33,893
For two x cubed plus x squared minus
thirteen x plus six, the candidates are

92
00:06:33,893 --> 00:06:38,446
plus or minus one, two, three, six, one
half and three halves.

93
00:06:39,566 --> 00:06:44,174
Test candidates with synthetic division
until the remainder is zero.

94
00:06:44,624 --> 00:06:50,128
Try two: the bottom row is two, five,
negative three, zero. So two is a zero.

95
00:06:51,266 --> 00:06:57,704
The quotient is two x squared plus five x
minus three, which factors as two x minus

96
00:06:57,704 --> 00:06:59,565
one, times x plus three.

97
00:07:00,015 --> 00:07:03,108
So the zeros are two, one half, and
negative three.

98
00:07:04,233 --> 00:07:07,497
A graph helps you guess which candidates
to test first.

99
00:07:07,947 --> 00:07:12,918
Look where the curve crosses the x axis,
then confirm with synthetic division.

100
00:07:14,066 --> 00:07:18,504
After you find some zeros, the leftover
quadratic may not factor.

101
00:07:18,954 --> 00:07:23,028
Use the quadratic formula for irrational
or complex zeros.

102
00:07:24,166 --> 00:07:30,406
Your turn. List the possible rational
zeros of x cubed minus two x squared minus

103
00:07:30,406 --> 00:07:31,654
five x plus six.

104
00:07:32,104 --> 00:07:33,640
Pause the video and try it.

105
00:07:34,090 --> 00:07:36,501
Plus or minus one, two, three, and six.

106
00:07:37,633 --> 00:07:43,319
How many zeros does a polynomial have? The
fundamental theorem of algebra answers

107
00:07:43,319 --> 00:07:43,670
that.

108
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:50,716
Every polynomial of degree n, with n at
least one, has exactly n complex zeros,

109
00:07:50,716 --> 00:07:51,989
counting repeats.

110
00:07:52,439 --> 00:07:54,764
A cubic has three, a quartic has four.

111
00:07:55,900 --> 00:08:00,991
A repeated zero counts as many times as
its factor appears. That's its

112
00:08:00,991 --> 00:08:01,937
multiplicity.

113
00:08:02,387 --> 00:08:08,927
x minus two, squared, times x plus one has
zeros two, two, and negative one: three

114
00:08:08,927 --> 00:08:09,406
zeros.

115
00:08:10,533 --> 00:08:14,864
For real coefficients, complex zeros come
in conjugate pairs.

116
00:08:15,314 --> 00:08:18,599
If three plus i is a zero, so is three
minus i.

117
00:08:19,733 --> 00:08:23,957
Write a polynomial with zeros one, and two
plus or minus i.

118
00:08:24,407 --> 00:08:31,306
The complex pair gives x squared minus
four x plus five. Multiply by x minus one:

119
00:08:31,306 --> 00:08:35,735
x cubed minus five x squared plus nine x
minus five.

120
00:08:36,866 --> 00:08:42,050
Since complex zeros pair up, a cubic
always has at least one real zero.

121
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:45,444
Its graph must cross the x axis at least
once.

122
00:08:46,566 --> 00:08:52,542
Your turn. A degree-four polynomial has
zeros three, negative one, and two i. What

123
00:08:52,542 --> 00:08:53,926
is the fourth zero?

124
00:08:54,376 --> 00:08:55,912
Pause the video and try it.

125
00:08:56,362 --> 00:08:59,349
Negative two i, the conjugate of two i.

126
00:09:00,466 --> 00:09:06,135
With zeros, multiplicity and end behavior,
you can sketch any factored polynomial.

127
00:09:06,135 --> 00:09:07,655
Let's put it together.

128
00:09:08,799 --> 00:09:15,413
Sketch f of x equals x plus two, times x
minus one, squared, times x minus three.

129
00:09:15,863 --> 00:09:19,063
First, plot the zeros: negative two, one,
and three.

130
00:09:20,199 --> 00:09:24,189
At a zero with odd multiplicity, the graph
crosses the axis.

131
00:09:24,639 --> 00:09:29,700
At a zero with even multiplicity, it
touches and turns back. So the graph

132
00:09:29,700 --> 00:09:30,740
bounces at one.

133
00:09:31,866 --> 00:09:36,793
The degree is four and the leading
coefficient is positive, so both ends

134
00:09:36,793 --> 00:09:37,135
rise.

135
00:09:38,266 --> 00:09:43,493
Start high on the left, come down and
cross at negative two, rise and bounce at

136
00:09:43,493 --> 00:09:46,735
one, dip back down, then cross at three
and rise.

137
00:09:47,185 --> 00:09:50,300
Between one and three, the graph dips
below the axis.

138
00:09:51,433 --> 00:09:57,076
The y intercept is f of zero: two times
one times negative three, which is

139
00:09:57,076 --> 00:09:58,067
negative six.

140
00:09:58,517 --> 00:10:00,224
That point confirms the shape.

141
00:10:01,366 --> 00:10:06,545
Your turn. Where does f of x equals x
squared times the quantity x minus four

142
00:10:06,545 --> 00:10:09,302
cross the axis, and where does it bounce?

143
00:10:09,752 --> 00:10:11,288
Pause the video and try it.

144
00:10:11,738 --> 00:10:16,282
It bounces at zero, which has multiplicity
two, and crosses at four.

145
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:22,821
In this last lesson of Unit Five, we'll
study polynomial identities: equations

146
00:10:22,821 --> 00:10:26,019
that are true for every value of the
variable.

147
00:10:27,167 --> 00:10:32,930
You already know some identities, like a
plus b, squared, equals a squared plus two

148
00:10:32,930 --> 00:10:34,249
a b plus b squared.

149
00:10:34,699 --> 00:10:37,131
An identity is true for every a and b.

150
00:10:38,267 --> 00:10:44,597
Two new ones: a cubed plus b cubed equals
a plus b, times a squared minus a b plus b

151
00:10:44,597 --> 00:10:45,200
squared.

152
00:10:45,650 --> 00:10:51,642
And a cubed minus b cubed equals a minus
b, times a squared plus a b plus b

153
00:10:51,642 --> 00:10:58,194
squared. x cubed minus eight is x minus
two, times x squared plus two x plus four.

154
00:10:59,333 --> 00:11:03,536
To prove an identity, expand one side
until it matches the other.

155
00:11:03,986 --> 00:11:10,322
Multiply a minus b by a squared plus a b
plus b squared. The middle terms cancel,

156
00:11:10,322 --> 00:11:12,669
leaving a cubed minus b cubed.

157
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:19,839
The identity x squared minus y squared,
squared, plus two x y, squared, equals x

158
00:11:19,839 --> 00:11:24,595
squared plus y squared, squared, generates
Pythagorean triples.

159
00:11:25,045 --> 00:11:28,970
With x equals two and y equals one: three,
four and five.

160
00:11:30,100 --> 00:11:35,343
Expanding a plus b to higher powers
follows a pattern of coefficients called

161
00:11:35,343 --> 00:11:36,585
Pascal's triangle.

162
00:11:37,035 --> 00:11:39,595
We'll use it later with the binomial
theorem.

163
00:11:40,733 --> 00:11:43,976
Your turn. Factor twenty-seven x cubed
plus one.

164
00:11:44,426 --> 00:11:45,962
Pause the video and try it.

165
00:11:46,412 --> 00:11:53,099
It's three x cubed plus one cubed: three x
plus one, times nine x squared minus three

166
00:11:53,099 --> 00:11:53,964
x plus one.

167
00:11:55,100 --> 00:12:00,262
That completes Unit Five. Next, we'll
study rational expressions and functions

168
00:12:00,262 --> 00:12:05,359
in Unit Six. If this helped, subscribe to
School Math, Explained, and turn on

169
00:12:05,359 --> 00:12:08,668
notifications so you don't miss it. See
you there.

